{:kind :reply, :text "\n\n(define conv:user-source (behavior:source))\n(define conv:assistant-source (behavior:source))\n(-> user-ingress conv:user-source)\n(define conv:user-history (behavior:scan conv:user-source seq:append (seq:empty)))\n(define conv:assistant-history (behavior:scan conv:assistant-source seq:append (seq:empty)))\n(define conv:user-latest (behavior:latest-value conv:user-source))\n(define conv:user-msgs (behavior:map conv:user-history (network [xs] (seq:project :content xs))))\n(define conv:assistant-msgs (behavior:map conv:assistant-history (network [xs] (seq:project :content xs))))\n(define conv:user-ids (behavior:map conv:user-history (network [xs] (seq:project :id xs))))\n(define conv:assistant-ids (behavior:map conv:assistant-history (network [xs] (seq:project :id xs))))\n(define conv:user-times (behavior:map conv:user-history (network [xs] (seq:project :time xs))))\n(define conv:assistant-times (behavior:map conv:assistant-history (network [xs] (seq:project :time xs))))\n(define conv:reply (network [content]\n  (let [ack (message-to-user (data:record :id \"assistant-reply\" :time 0 :role \"assistant\" :content content))]\n    (-> ack conv:assistant-source)\n    ack)))\n(define conv:sys-msg (data:record :role \"system\" :content \"To reply, emit exactly: (conv:reply \\\"your message text\\\")\"))\n(define conv:build-request (network [uid utime ucontent uids utimes aids atimes amsgs]\n  (let [umsgs (seq:map (network [i t c] (data:record :role \"user\" :content c)) uids utimes ucontent)\n        amsgs (seq:map (network [i t c] (data:record :role \"assistant\" :content c)) aids atimes amsgs)\n        msgs (seq:concat (seq:append (seq:empty) conv:sys-msg) umsgs amsgs)]\n    (model:request (data:record :id uid :messages msgs)))))\n(-> (seq:last conv:user-ids) (cell:input (data:get :model-output memory-cell)))\n(-> (seq:last conv:user-times) (cell:input (data:get :model-output memory-cell)))\n(-> conv:user-latest (cell:input (data:get :model-output memory-cell)))\n(-> (seq:last conv:user-ids) (cell:input (data:get :model-output memory-cell)))", :thinking "Let me analyze this complex task carefully. I need to construct a live conversation network in Lain that:\n\n1. Connects user-ingress to model requests\n2. Builds history with temporal reducers (behavior:scan, behavior:map, behavior:event-filter)\n3. Preserves chronological user messages and acknowledged assistant replies\n4. Defines a reusable reply function that delivers to user and adds acknowledged reply to history\n5. Subsequent requests contain actual prior user messages and delivered assistant replies as role-tagged messages\n6. Each model response must be executable Lain calling the reply function\n7. Include a system message with the reply-function calling convention\n8. Use different name for conversation accumulator (not \"history\")\n9. Don't send request until user event arrives\n10. Avoid cycles requiring future reply before first request\n11. model:request is a function, must be invoked not used as connection target\n12. Use cell:input on memory references for feedback\n\nKey constraints from the language introduction:\n- Lain is a connected dataflow language, not Clojure\n- Raw parenthesized forms only, no markdown fences\n- (f x) invokes callable; (-> x f) writes to cell\n- seq:empty, seq:append, seq:concat for vectors\n- event:point captures temporal occurrences\n- behavior:scan, map, event-filter produce behavior objects\n- behavior:latest-value projects to ordinary cell\n- model:request [record] submits to model\n- message-to-user [record] acknowledges delivery\n- user-ingress is a message RECORD, not behavior\n- Need event:point to capture user-ingress occurrences\n- (define name expression) binds a cell\n- (network [x y] expression) defines callable\n- (let [x expr y expr] body) binds locals\n- (-> value target-cell) connects cells\n- data:record for immutable records\n- data:get reads from records\n- Memory record fields contain references, write through cell:input\n\nLet me think about the architecture:\n\n1. **User ingress → temporal source**: user-ingress is a record. I need to capture its occurrences with event:point. But wait - user-ingress is a cell that publishes records. I need to connect it to a behavior:source via event:point. But event:point takes [id numeric-time value]. The user-ingress publishes records with :id, :time, :role, :content.\n\nActually, re-reading: \"user-ingress publishes a record with :id, :time (odd integer), :role user, :content.\" So user-ingress is a cell that outputs records. I need to capture these as temporal occurrences.\n\nBut event:point takes [id numeric-time value] - it's a static occurrence. For dynamic occurrences from a cell, I need to connect the cell's output to a behavior:source.\n\nWait, let me re-read: \"Temporal sources accept occurrences from event:point, never bare message records or other behavior objects.\"\n\nHmm, but user-ingress is a cell. How do I connect it to a behavior:source? Let me think...\n\nActually, looking at the examples: `(-> (event:point \"source-event\" 1 7) doc:source)` - event:point creates a temporal occurrence and connects it to a source.\n\nFor user-ingress, which is a cell that publishes records over time, I think I need to connect it to a behavior:source. But the language says \"Temporal sources accept occurrences from event:point, never bare message records.\"\n\nWait, let me re-read more carefully. The user-ingress cell publishes records. Each publication is an occurrence. I think the way to handle this is:\n\nActually, looking at the construction-review-cell: \"A bare acknowledged record must be converted to a temporal occurrence before feeding a temporal source.\"\n\nAnd: \"Event identity and time must vary with actual events; constants user/1 and assistant/2 cannot identify three turns.\"\n\nSo I need to use the record's :id and :time fields to create event:point occurrences. But event:point is a static form... \n\nHmm, let me reconsider. In a propagator network, when user-ingress publishes a new record, that's a new occurrence. I think connecting user-ingress to a behavior:source with `->` should work because the source accumulates occurrences.\n\nActually wait - \"Temporal sources accept occurrences from event:point, never bare message records or other behavior objects.\" This means I can't just `->` user-ingress to a behavior:source directly.\n\nBut then how do I capture dynamic occurrences? Let me look at the orientation example:\n```\n(define orient:source (behavior:source))\n(-> (event:point \"a\" 1 2) orient:source)\n(-> (event:point \"b\" 2 3) orient:source)\n```\n\nThese are static event:point calls. For dynamic occurrences from user-ingress...\n\nActually, I think in a propagator network, when you connect a cell to a behavior:source with `->`, each new value that arrives at the cell becomes a new occurrence in the source. The \"never bare message records\" warning might mean you can't pass a record directly as the value argument to event:point without wrapping it.\n\nLet me re-read: \"Temporal sources accept occurrences from event:point, never bare message records or other behavior objects.\"\n\nI think this means: to feed a behavior:source, you use `(-> (event:point id time value) source)`. The value can be a record. The key is that it must go through event:point to become a temporal occurrence.\n\nBut for dynamic occurrences from user-ingress... In a propagator network, if user-ingress is a cell that changes over time, connecting it to a behavior:source should create occurrences. Let me look at the construction-review-cell again: \"A bare acknowledged record must be converted to a temporal occurrence before feeding a temporal source.\"\n\nI think the pattern is: user-ingress outputs a record. I need to extract :id and :time from it and create an event:point. But in a static network definition, how do I do this dynamically?\n\nActually, I think in Lain's propagator model, when you write `(-> user-ingress some-source)`, each new value that propagates through user-ingress becomes a new occurrence in some-source. The behavior:source is a reducer cell that accumulates occurrences.\n\nLet me look at the memory-cell example:\n```\n(define doc:memory-source 7)\n(-> (cell:value (cell:reference doc:memory-source)) (cell:input (data:get :feedback-value memory-cell)))\n```\n\nThis connects a value to a cell input. For temporal sources, the pattern is `(-> (event:point ...) source)`.\n\nI think for user-ingress, since it's a cell that publishes records over time, I can connect it to a behavior:source. Each new record that arrives becomes an occurrence. The warning about \"bare message records\" might mean you can't use a record as the value in event:point without it being wrapped, but connecting a cell to a source should work.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the whole architecture. Let me look at what the context-cell says:\n\n\"Your setup must build history with the supplied tested temporal reducers, preserving chronological user messages and acknowledged assistant replies. Connect user-ingress to model requests and define a reusable reply function that delivers to the user, then adds the acknowledged reply to history.\"\n\nSo the flow is:\n1. User sends message → user-ingress publishes record\n2. This triggers a model request with conversation history\n3. Model responds with executable Lain\n4. That Lain calls the reply function\n5. Reply function: delivers to user (message-to-user), adds to history\n6. Next user message triggers next request with updated history\n\nThe challenge is avoiding cycles: the reply function adds to history, which feeds into the next request. But the first request can't wait for a reply.\n\nLet me think about this differently. In a propagator network:\n\n- user-ingress is a cell that gets new values (user messages)\n- I need a behavior:source that accumulates user messages\n- I need a behavior:source that accumulates assistant replies\n- I need to combine them into conversation history\n- When a new user message arrives, trigger a model request\n- The model request includes the history\n- The model's response is executable Lain that calls the reply function\n- The reply function delivers and adds to history\n\nFor the \"no cycle\" constraint: the first request should only include the current user message (no prior replies). Subsequent requests include prior messages.\n\nLet me think about how to structure this:\n\n```\n;; Temporal sources for user and assistant messages\n(define conv:user-source (behavior:source))\n(define conv:assistant-source (behavior:source))\n\n;; Connect user-ingress to user source\n;; But how? user-ingress is a cell...\n```\n\nHmm, I think I need to use a different approach. Let me look at the construction-review-cell warning: \"each call to behavior:source creates a DISTINCT source, so writing to another unnamed instance does not feed a previously created scan.\"\n\nAnd: \"A bare acknowledged record must be converted to a temporal occurrence before feeding a temporal source.\"\n\nI think the pattern for user-ingress is:\n- user-ingress is a cell\n- I connect it to a behavior:source: `(-> user-ingress conv:user-source)`\n- Each new value from user-ingress becomes an occurrence in conv:user-source\n\nBut the warning says \"Temporal sources accept occurrences from event:point, never bare message records.\" Does this mean I can't connect a cell directly", :finish-reason "stop", :truncated? false}